#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134940} :activity/id #uuid "a09fce56-8cba-5c04-b594-13a87263e94e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048142 :topic/id #uuid "82d65d50-f769-531a-8e21-07640e0f163f" :topic/math-academy-id 2215} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134940 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135016 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we're given the following **explicit formula** for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\na_n = 2\\cdot 5^n, \\qquad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nWe can write this geometric sequence using a recursive formula. Recall that the recursive formula for *any* geometric sequence is\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = r\\cdot a_n\n$$\n\nTo convert from the explicit formula to the recursive formula, we just need to work out the first term and common ratio. The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{1} &= 2 \\cdot 5^{1} = 10 \\\\\na_{2} &= 2 \\cdot 5^{2} = 50\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\frac{a_{2}}{a_{1}} = \\frac{50}{10} = 5\n$$\n\nSo, the first term is\n\n$$\na_1={\\color{blue}10}\n$$\n\nand the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr={\\color{red}5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the recursive formula is as follows:\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = {\\color{red}5} \\cdot a_n, \\qquad a_1 = {\\color{blue}10}, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3cb2e1b6-42f6-5508-aa68-1085dd64e89c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1670 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d53098fa-92a8-5d13-9bb6-afc94451163e" :step/math-academy-id 459983 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134941}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134941 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133932 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133550 :question/id #uuid "6aa991c4-28e3-51fc-9642-19fa9346202d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4237"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f258f110-e541-5edf-8050-54d03177e499" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048829}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134445}] :knowledge-point/title "Translating From the Explicit to the Recursive Formula"} :step/id #uuid "28a0a6a2-8445-5dbf-bab4-94273d8bd5a2" :step/math-academy-id 459984 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134942}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134942 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133933 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133536 :question/id #uuid "5537647b-d722-5000-8445-b2743cc1f1f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-1512"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6af17da8-272b-55fb-8a5d-5857bd41051e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050067}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134175}] :knowledge-point/title "Translating From the Explicit to the Recursive Formula Using Function Notation"} :step/id #uuid "842bc2ad-3287-51df-b12a-9c624c1f5178" :step/math-academy-id 459987 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134943}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134943 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135017 :tutorial/content "It's often more useful to translate from the recursive formula to an explicit one. So how do we find the explicit formula for the geometric sequence defined recursively as\n\n$$\na_{n+1} ={\\color{red}\\dfrac 1 4} a_n,\\, \\qquad a_1 = {\\color{blue}2},\\, \\qquad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nThe explicit formula for a geometric sequence is\n\n$$\na_n = a_1 \\cdot r^{n-1}, \\quad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nso we just need to determine the first term $a_1$ and the common ratio $r$.\n\nWe are given that the first term is\n\n$$\na_1={\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nTo find the common ratio, we compare the given sequence with the general recursive formula for a geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n + 1} &= r \\cdot a_{n} \\\\\na_{n + 1} &= \\frac{1}{4}a_{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the above, we see that\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}r} = {\\color{red}\\dfrac 1 4}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute\n\n$$\na_1={\\color{blue}2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nr={\\color{red}\\dfrac{1}{4}}\n$$\n\ninto the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_n &= a_1 \\cdot r^{n-1} \\\\ &= {\\color{blue}2} \\cdot \\left({\\color{red}\\dfrac{1}{4}} \\right)^{n-1} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\na_n=2 \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4} \\right)^{n-1}, \\quad n \\geq 1\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d326c47a-d9db-5669-be98-4bee64971fd7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1671 :tutorial/title "Translating From the Recursive to the Explicit Formula"} :step/id #uuid "5d1a36ce-aed5-518c-95d0-8d09a832322f" :step/math-academy-id 459990 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134944}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134944 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133934 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133537 :question/id #uuid "19d183f9-cf05-5477-8da9-642e2fd8e03b" :question/math-academy-id "e-1513"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac7a8723-02b7-5467-98f7-530ba8239c4f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134444}] :knowledge-point/title "Translating From the Recursive to the Explicit Formula"} :step/id #uuid "d3301f00-0418-594d-8113-7d7d6804a694" :step/math-academy-id 459991 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134945}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134945 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133935 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133551 :question/id #uuid "5027a265-fd62-574b-b6fa-2bacf9de7304" :question/math-academy-id "e-4238"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a910df9-7b4a-5892-a084-52d8b5dcd086" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134478}] :knowledge-point/title "Translating From the Recursive to the Explicit Formula Using Function Notation"} :step/id #uuid "3c4a6c1b-5e7f-529b-9fd1-f8e9ae72b8ed" :step/math-academy-id 459994}] :activity/title "Translating Between Explicit and Recursive Formulas for Geometric Sequences" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134050}]}
